% ============================================================================
% synonyms — a common word and EVERY word the source's Synonyms line lists
% for it that means the same thing (synonym) (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling library to `opposites.adj`: many words come in pairs that mean
% the SAME thing, not the opposite. HAPPY and CHEERFUL both describe feeling
% good. The word for "means the same as" is a SYNONYM. This tiny library
% records clean word→synonym pairs so an ADJ program can recall them, each
% one cited to a dictionary. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? synonym(happy, $Word)      % cheerful ; content ; delighted ; ...
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `synonym(word, synonym)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns EVERY
% synonym WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and ABSTAINS on
% any word not in the table (it never invents a synonym it cannot cite). Each
% word is deliberately ONE-TO-MANY: the source's own Synonyms line for each
% word names several synonyms, not just one, so `synonym(happy, $Word)` binds
% `$Word` to EACH of them in turn — the same single-valued-to-many-valued
% shape `kingdoms.adj` and `flame-colors.adj` already established.
%
% The pairs, as a truth table:
%
%     word    synonym          read as…
%     ------  ---------------  ------------------------------
%     happy   cheerful         "a synonym of happy is cheerful"
%     happy   content
%     happy   delighted
%     happy   elated
%     happy   exultant
%     happy   glad
%     happy   joyful
%     happy   jubilant
%     happy   merry
%     smart   bright           "a synonym of smart is bright"
%     smart   capable
%     smart   sophisticated
%     smart   witty
%     quick   fast             "a synonym of quick is fast"
%     quick   speedy
%     quick   rapid
%     quick   swift
%
% Only ONE direction is shipped per pair (word → its synonym). A query like
% `? synonym(cheerful, $W)` therefore ABSTAINS: the honest-abstention property
% is deliberate — the table answers only what its source explicitly lists,
% and a word with no shipped synonym (e.g. `purple`) returns no invented
% answer. (This mirrors `opposites.adj`'s own one-direction convention.)
%
% Provenance — nothing here is from memory. Each pair is copied from the
% English Wiktionary entry for the FIRST word, whose "Synonyms" line lists
% the synonyms verbatim. The per-word citable spans (quoted exactly as
% printed) are:
%
%     happy  → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/happy  "cheerful, content, delighted, elated, exultant, glad, joyful, jubilant, merry"
%     smart  → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smart  "bright, capable, sophisticated, witty"  (sense: exhibiting social ability or cleverness)
%     quick  → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quick  "fast, speedy, rapid, swift"  (sense: moving with speed)
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 3 to 17 rows: the header ABOVE already quoted the
% full Synonyms line for each word when this table originally shipped — only
% the FIRST synonym in each line had ever been turned into a row, even though
% the same already-quoted span names eight more (happy), three more (smart),
% and three more (quick). Zero new source page needed; WebFetch re-verified
% all three Wiktionary "Synonyms" lines live before writing (confirming each
% word's already-quoted span is accurate, unchanged, and, for `smart`/`quick`,
% still scoped to the single sense already named in this header). This
% mirrors `kingdoms.adj`'s single-to-many-valued-per-key extension shape,
% but needed no new WebFetch to DISCOVER the extra values — they were already
% sitting in this table's own header the whole time.
%
% The single `source` span below is the clearest one (the `happy` entry); the
% `locator` points at that entry, and each remaining word's own page + verbatim
% span is quoted above so every row is independently checkable. `trust
% consensus` — a dictionary is a secondary reference, not a primary authority,
% the same tier `opposites.adj` already established for the same Wiktionary
% source. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table synonym {
    columns word, synonym

    row (happy, cheerful)
    row (happy, content)
    row (happy, delighted)
    row (happy, elated)
    row (happy, exultant)
    row (happy, glad)
    row (happy, joyful)
    row (happy, jubilant)
    row (happy, merry)
    row (smart, bright)
    row (smart, capable)
    row (smart, sophisticated)
    row (smart, witty)
    row (quick, fast)
    row (quick, speedy)
    row (quick, rapid)
    row (quick, swift)

    source "cheerful, content, delighted, elated, exultant, glad, joyful, jubilant, merry"
    locator "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/happy"
    trust consensus
}
